Doctoral Research Proposal

Theoretical Foundations of a Ground–Orbital Electromagnetic Plasma Platform (GOEPP): Weighted-Space Existence Theory, Regime Non-Conflation, and Architectural Exclusion Principles

Field: Applied Electromagnetics, Plasma Physics, Mathematical Physics

Nature of Work: Purely theoretical and analytical. No experimental apparatus is constructed, no physical test is performed, and no computational code is trained or executed. All results are derived analytically from first principles and bounded against published measured data. A separate section describes future implementation steps that would follow from the theoretical results, without performing them.

Status: Grounded in established physical laws and published experimental benchmarks. Speculative mechanisms excluded.

---

Abstract

This proposal establishes a purely theoretical doctoral research programme for the mathematical formalisation and analytical bounding of a Ground–Orbital Electromagnetic Plasma Platform (GOEPP). The work is organised around a Mathematical Non-Conflation Principle: four physically distinct subsystems—a toroidal magnetic confinement configuration, a laser-filament-guided weakly conductive plasma column, a near-field resonant wireless power transfer array, and a far-field orbital microwave power-beaming link—are each governed by a separate mathematical framework with distinct dimensionless governing parameters. No coupling between non-adjacent regimes is asserted.

The formal results include: (i) a weighted-space existence theorem for the Grad–Shafranov equation in H^1_{0,R}(\Omega; R^{-1}dR\,dZ), with coercivity established via Berestycki–Brezis theory and calibrated against the multiple-solution results of Ham and Farrell (2024) and Pentland et al. (2025); (ii) a coupled-mode efficiency bound for two identical resonators expressed in terms of the strong-coupling factor U = k\sqrt{Q_1 Q_2}, derived from both coupled-mode theory and Kirchhoff circuit equations, with an explicit Lemma of Non-Applicability for the far-field regime d > \lambda/2\pi; (iii) a plasma dielectric exclusion theorem establishing that electromagnetic penetration into a 10^{16}–10^{17} cm⁻³ plasma column at 100–500 kHz is bounded by a skin depth \delta < 10^{-4} m, rendering tower–plasma coupling analytically zero; (iv) a filamentation length bound derived from multiphoton/tunnel ionisation loss rates, consistent with the established scaling L_f \propto \lambda a_0^{-2} \sqrt{P/P_{\text{crit}}}; and (v) a Gaussian-beam collection-efficiency theorem with a three-tier efficiency budget calibrated against the Caltech SSPP system-level analysis. The primary novelty lies in the rigorous non-conflation of the four mathematical regimes and the systematic derivation of architectural exclusion principles.

