2. Mathematical Framework and Formal Results

2.1 Function Spaces and Coercivity

Definition 2.1 (Weighted Sobolev Space). Let \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2 be a bounded Lipschitz domain with R \geq R_{\min} > 0 (tokamak geometry). Define

H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) = \left\{ \psi : \Omega \to \mathbb{R} \;\middle|\; \|\psi\|_{H^1_{0,R}}^2 = \int_\Omega R^{-1} |\nabla \psi|^2 \, dR\,dZ < \infty, \; \psi|_{\partial\Omega} = 0 \right\}.

The associated bilinear form is a(\psi, \phi) = \int_\Omega R^{-1} \nabla \psi \cdot \nabla \phi \, dR\,dZ.

Coercivity and Norm Equivalence. The weighted Poincaré inequality

\|\psi\|_{L^2_R} \leq C(\Omega, R_{\min}) \|\nabla \psi\|_{L^2_R}

holds for \psi \in H^1_{0,R}(\Omega). Since R \geq R_{\min} > 0 in the tokamak domain, the weight R^{-1} is bounded away from zero and infinity, so H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) is norm-equivalent to the standard H^1_0(\Omega). The bilinear form a(\cdot,\cdot) is coercive with constant \alpha^* = \alpha^*(\Omega, R_{\min}) > 0. This framework follows Temam (1977) and Berestycki–Brezis (1980), who established general uniqueness results for Grad–Shafranov-type equations with globally Lipschitz nonlinearities.

2.2 Grad–Shafranov Equation: Weighted-Space Existence and Non-Uniqueness

Theorem 2.1 (Weighted-Space Existence). Let \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2 be a bounded Lipschitz domain. Suppose p(\psi) \in C^1(\mathbb{R}) and F(\psi) \in C^1(\mathbb{R}) satisfy the monotonicity conditions

p'(\psi) \leq 0, \qquad F(\psi) F'(\psi) \leq 0

for all \psi in the range of \psi, and that p', (F^2)' \in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}). Then there exists a weak solution \psi \in H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) to the Grad–Shafranov equation

-\Delta^* \psi = \mu_0 R^2 \frac{dp}{d\psi} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{dF^2}{d\psi}.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds via the Leray–Schauder fixed-point theorem applied to the map

T = (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} \circ \mathcal{N} : H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) \to H^1_{0,R}(\Omega),

where \mathcal{N}: \psi \mapsto -\mu_0 R^2 p'(\psi) - \frac{1}{2}(F^2)'(\psi) is the Nemytskii operator associated with the nonlinear source term, and (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} is the inverse of the linearised Grad–Shafranov operator with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions.

Step 1: Boundedness of \mathcal{N}. Under the L^\infty assumption on p' and (F^2)', the Nemytskii operator maps H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) boundedly into L^2_R(\Omega).

Step 2: Elliptic regularity. For the linearised operator -\Delta^*_0 with C^{2,\alpha} boundary, the inverse (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} maps L^2_R(\Omega) into H^2(\Omega) \cap H^1_{0,R}(\Omega). By the Rellich–Kondrachov theorem, the embedding H^2(\Omega) \cap H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) \hookrightarrow H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) is compact.

Step 3: A priori bound. Using the coercivity of a(\cdot,\cdot) with constant \alpha^*, the variational form with test function \phi = T\psi yields

\alpha^* \|T\psi\|_{H^1_{0,R}}^2 \leq a(T\psi, T\psi) = \int_\Omega T\psi \, \mathcal{N}(\psi) \, dR\,dZ \leq \|\mathcal{N}(\psi)\|_{L^2_R} \|T\psi\|_{L^2_R}.

Combined with the weighted Poincaré inequality, this bounds T\psi in H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) uniformly for \psi in a ball M \subset H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) of radius r_0. The Leray–Schauder theorem then yields a fixed point \psi \in M. ∎

Theorem 2.1b (Non-Uniqueness Calibration). Let \mathcal{F}(\psi) = C_1 - \tanh((\psi - \psi_p)/w) be the nonlinear source profile adopted in the large-aspect-ratio model of Ham and Farrell. Then there exists a critical value C^* \approx 2.7 such that:

· For C < C^*, the trivial solution \psi \equiv 0 is the unique solution;
· For C > C^*, there exist at least three distinct solutions: the trivial solution and two non-trivial solutions, arising from a fold bifurcation.

This result is established in Ham and Farrell, Nucl. Fusion 64 (2024) 034001, which states that "the Grad–Shafranov equation for axisymmetric MHD equilibria is a nonlinear, scalar PDE which in principle can have zero, one or more non-trivial solutions" and develops "a simple analytic model to calculate multiple solutions in the large aspect ratio limit". The follow-up work by Pentland et al., Nucl. Fusion 65 (2025) 086053, discovers "multiple solutions to the static forward free-boundary GS problem in the MAST-U tokamak geometry using the validated evolutive equilibrium solver FreeGSNKE and the deflated continuation algorithm".

Remark 2.2 (Physical Implications of Non-Uniqueness). The existence of multiple equilibrium branches has direct consequences for the GOEPP architecture: a configuration may be stable in one equilibrium branch and unstable in another. The number of available branches depends on the profile shapes and boundary conditions. The GOEPP toroidal core must therefore be designed with explicit branch selection criteria.

Theorem 2.2 (Solov'ev Exact Solution as Benchmark). For linear profiles p(\psi) = p_0 + p_1\psi and F^2(\psi) = F_0 + F_1\psi, the Grad–Shafranov equation admits the exact analytical solution

\psi(R, Z) = \frac{p_1}{2} R^4 + A R^2 + B + C R^2 Z^2 + \sum_k c_k \psi_k(R, Z),

where A, B, C are determined by the profile coefficients and \psi_k are homogeneous solutions of the linearised operator. This solution is unique within the Solov'ev class for prescribed boundary data. The proof follows by substitution and the maximum principle applied to the linearised operator.

Remark 2.3 (Positioning of the Solov'ev Solution). The Solov'ev solution is classical and well-known. It is not claimed as a novel uniqueness theorem. Its role is as an analytical benchmark against which the nonlinear solution can be compared.

2.3 Coupled-Mode Theory: Corrected Efficiency Bound and Regime Dichotomy

Theorem 2.3 (Two-Resonator Efficiency Bound). For two identical resonators with resonant frequency \omega, loss rate \Gamma = \omega/(2Q), and coupling coefficient \kappa, the maximum power transfer efficiency under optimal load is

\eta_{\max} = \frac{U^2}{\left(1 + \sqrt{1 + U^2}\right)^2},

where U = \kappa/\Gamma = k\sqrt{Q_1 Q_2} is the strong-coupling factor and k = M/\sqrt{L_1 L_2} is the coupling coefficient.

In the strong-coupling regime (U \gg 1), \eta_{\max} \to 1. In the weak-coupling regime (U \ll 1), \eta_{\max} \approx U^2/4. This is standard WPT theory, demonstrated experimentally by Kurs et al., Science 317 (2007) 83–86, who transferred "60 watts with approximately 40% efficiency over distances in excess of 2 meters".

Lemma 2.1 (Regime Dichotomy — Formal Non-Conflation). Let d_{\text{NF}} = \lambda/2\pi.

· If d < d_{\text{NF}} and Q > 100: k is defined, efficiency given by Theorem 2.3.
· If d > d_{\text{NF}}: k is undefined, power transfer governed by Friis

P_r = P_t G_t G_r \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi L}\right)^2,

and collection efficiency by Theorem 2.6.

No interpolation between regimes is permitted. This lemma is the formal statement of the Mathematical Non-Conflation Principle for near-field and far-field regimes.

Corollary 2.1 (Distance Scaling). For coaxial loops with radius r separated by distance d \gg r, the mutual inductance scales as M \propto r^4/d^3, hence k \propto r^3/d^3 and, in the weak-coupling regime, \eta \propto (kQ)^2 \propto Q^2 r^6/d^6.

2.4 Plasma Dielectric: Quantitative Exclusion with Collisional Correction

Theorem 2.4 (Quantitative Plasma Exclusion). Consider a plasma column with electron density n_e = 10^{16}–10^{17} cm⁻³ = 10^{22}–10^{23} m⁻³ and electron-neutral collision frequency \nu_{en} \sim 10^9 s⁻¹. The plasma frequency is

\omega_p = \sqrt{\frac{n_e e^2}{\varepsilon_0 m_e}} \approx 5.6 \times 10^{12} \text{ rad/s} \quad (n_e = 10^{22} \text{ m}^{-3}).

For a ground tower operating at f = 100–500 kHz, \omega_{\text{tower}} = 6.28 \times 10^5–3.14 \times 10^6 rad/s. The ratio \omega_{\text{tower}}/\omega_p < 10^{-4}. Under these conditions, the plasma dielectric function

\varepsilon(\omega) = 1 - \frac{\omega_p^2}{\omega(\omega + j\nu)}

is negative and large in magnitude: \varepsilon \approx -\omega_p^2/\omega^2 \approx -10^{14}. The electromagnetic penetration depth is

\delta = \frac{c}{\sqrt{\omega_p^2 - \omega^2}} \approx \frac{c}{\omega_p} \approx 50 \;\mu\text{m}.

Therefore, the power coupling through a plasma column of thickness d \gg \delta is bounded by \exp(-2d/\delta), which is analytically zero.

Collisional Correction. The DC conductivity is \sigma_{\text{dc}} = n_e e^2/(m_e \nu) \approx 2.8 S/m for \nu = 10^9 s⁻¹. For E \sim few V/m at 1 m from a 13.56 MHz tower, P_{\text{abs}} \sim mW vs P_{\text{tower}} \sim kW, giving

\frac{P_{\text{abs}}}{P_{\text{tower}}} < 10^{-6}.

Exclusion is quantitative, not merely cutoff-based. Any architecture asserting tower-driven plasma sustainment at 100–500 kHz violates the cold-plasma cutoff condition and is analytically excluded.

2.5 Laser Filamentation: Corrected K and P_crit

Theorem 2.5 (Filamentation Length Bound). For 1030 nm, photon energy 1.2 eV, O₂ ionisation 12.07 eV: K = \lceil 12.07/1.2 \rceil = 11. Critical power P_{\text{crit}} = \lambda^2/(2\pi n_0 n_2) \approx 5.3 GW for 7 ps pulses at 1030 nm. The bound is

L_f \leq \frac{U_0}{\bar{\beta} I_0^{K-1}}.

This is consistent with the established scaling law L_f \propto \lambda a_0^{-2} \sqrt{P/P_{\text{crit}}} from the filamentation literature (Couairon & Mysyrowicz, Phys. Rep. 441 (2007) 47–189).

Conjecture 2.6. A 4 \times 4 array extends effective reservoir to ~16 U_0, projection L \leq 20 m under incoherent addition. This requires proof of no destructive interference — stated as conjecture, not theorem.

2.6 Orbital Beaming: Consistent Gaussian Treatment

Theorem 2.6 (Gaussian Collection).

\eta_{\text{coll}} = 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{2a_r^2}{w^2(L)}\right),

where w(L) = w_0\sqrt{1 + (\lambda L/\pi w_0^2)^2}.

For D_t = 10 m, \lambda = 5.17 cm (5.8 GHz): w(L) = L\theta/\sqrt{2} \approx 2.45 km at 550 km, where \theta = 1.22 \lambda/D_t = 6.3 mrad. For 90% collection: a_r > 1.07 w = 2.6 km.

