5. Novelty Assessment and Positioning

The individual theorems in this proposal are largely classical or elementary. The weighted-space existence theorem for the Grad–Shafranov equation is standard Berestycki–Brezis–Temam theory. The coupled-mode efficiency bound has been derived repeatedly since Kurs et al. (2007). The plasma dielectric criterion is elementary plasma physics. The filamentation bound is a first-order estimate consistent with earlier analytic models. The Gaussian-beam collection-efficiency theorem is the standard integral of a Gaussian intensity profile.

The genuine novelty of the programme lies in three synthesising elements:

1. Rigorous non-conflation of the four mathematical regimes, formalised through the governing dimensionless parameters \beta, U, \omega/\omega_p, and N_F, and the explicit Lemma of Non-Applicability.

2. The quantitative plasma exclusion principle, which rigorously excludes any claim of electromagnetic coupling between a 100–500 kHz tower and a 10^{16}–10^{17} cm⁻³ plasma column, with P_{\text{abs}}/P_{\text{tower}} < 10^{-6}.

3. Systematic derivation of analytical bounds calibrated against published data, establishing what is mathematically possible—and what is rigorously excluded—before any experimental platform is contemplated.

---

6. Open Mathematical Questions

1. Free-boundary uniqueness under weaker conditions. The existence theorem is stated for the fixed-boundary problem. The free-boundary problem requires additional regularity assumptions and a separate existence proof.

2. Rigorous multi-filament energy accounting. The energy-depletion bound is derived for a single filament. A rigorous treatment of multi-filament arrays requires accounting for energy exchange between filaments and the background reservoir.

3. Optimisation of the end-to-end efficiency product under ITU constraints. The optimisation of the efficiency product subject to ITU-R regulatory constraints on power density, frequency allocation, and orbital debris mitigation is an open problem in systems analysis.

4. Stability of multiple equilibrium branches. While the existence of multiple solutions to the Grad–Shafranov equation has been established, the stability of each branch and the conditions under which a configuration transitions between branches remain open questions.

