8. Conclusion

This proposal establishes a purely theoretical doctoral research programme for the mathematical formalisation of a Ground–Orbital Electromagnetic Plasma Platform. No experimental apparatus is constructed, no physical test is performed, and no computational code is trained or executed. The work derives, from first principles, the governing equations, existence and non-uniqueness conditions in weighted Sobolev spaces, efficiency bounds, propagation criteria, and scaling laws for four physically distinct subsystems. The primary novelty lies in the rigorous non-conflation of the four mathematical regimes and the systematic derivation of architectural exclusion principles. The theoretical nature of the work is not a limitation but a discipline: by deriving bounds analytically and calibrating against published data, the programme establishes what is mathematically possible—and what is rigorously excluded—before any experimental platform is contemplated.

Viva Defence Script.

Q: "What's new? These bounds are known."

A: The bounds are classical by design. The novelty is the systematic proof that the four regimes cannot be coupled: a 100 kHz tower cannot sustain 10^{22} m⁻³ plasma because \omega/\omega_p < 10^{-4} gives \delta < 10^{-4} m and P_{\text{abs}}/P_{\text{tower}} < 10^{-6}; the near-field U bound cannot be extrapolated to 550 km because d_{\text{NF}} = \lambda/2\pi; and Grad–Shafranov admits multiple branches at C > 2.7 per Ham & Farrell (2024) and Pentland et al. (2025), so branch selection is mandatory. The thesis is an exclusion principle thesis, not a feasibility thesis.


Appendices

Appendix A: Weighted Sobolev Spaces and the Grad–Shafranov Operator

The functional-analytic treatment of the Grad–Shafranov equation requires a weighted Sobolev space adapted to the cylindrical geometry of a toroidal domain. The operator

\Delta^* \psi = R \frac{\partial}{\partial R}\left( \frac{1}{R} \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial R} \right) + \frac{\partial^2 \psi}{\partial Z^2}

is not coercive on the standard space H^1_0(\Omega) equipped with Lebesgue measure dR\,dZ. The natural energy space is the weighted Sobolev space

H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) = \left\{ \psi : \Omega \to \mathbb{R} \;\middle|\; \|\psi\|_{H^1_{0,R}}^2 = \int_\Omega R^{-1} |\nabla \psi|^2 \, dR\,dZ < \infty, \; \psi|_{\partial\Omega} = 0 \right\},

equipped with the bilinear form a(\psi, \phi) = \int_\Omega R^{-1} \nabla \psi \cdot \nabla \phi \, dR\,dZ. In tokamak geometry, the major radius satisfies R \geq R_{\min} > 0, so the weight R^{-1} is bounded away from zero and infinity, and the weighted Poincaré inequality

\|\psi\|_{L^2_R} \leq C(\Omega, R_{\min}) \|\nabla \psi\|_{L^2_R}

holds. Consequently, H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) is norm-equivalent to H^1_0(\Omega), and a(\cdot,\cdot) is coercive with constant \alpha^* = \alpha^*(\Omega, R_{\min}) > 0. The standard references for this framework are Temam, Navier–Stokes Equations: Theory and Numerical Analysis (North-Holland, 1977), and Berestycki and Brezis, Nonlinear Anal. 4 (1980), 415–432. Berestycki and Brezis established general uniqueness results for Grad–Shafranov-type equations with globally Lipschitz nonlinearities, and their work is explicitly cited as the foundational uniqueness reference in the subsequent multiple-solution literature.

The existence proof for the weak solution proceeds via the Leray–Schauder fixed-point theorem applied to the map

T = (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} \circ \mathcal{N} : H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) \to H^1_{0,R}(\Omega),

where \mathcal{N}(\psi) = -\mu_0 R^2 p'(\psi) - \frac{1}{2}(F^2)'(\psi) is the Nemytskii operator associated with the nonlinear source term, and (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} is the inverse of the linearised Grad–Shafranov operator with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. The a priori bound follows from coercivity:

\alpha^* \|T\psi\|_{H^1_{0,R}}^2 \leq a(T\psi, T\psi) = \int_\Omega T\psi \, \mathcal{N}(\psi) \, dR\,dZ \leq \|\mathcal{N}(\psi)\|_{L^2_R} \|T\psi\|_{L^2_R}.

Combined with the weighted Poincaré inequality, this bounds T\psi in H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) uniformly for \psi in a ball M \subset H^1_{0,R}(\Omega) of radius r_0. The Leray–Schauder theorem then yields a fixed point \psi \in M. The Schauder fixed-point theorem requires that T: M \to M be a compact operator on a closed, convex, non-empty subset M of a Banach space; the compactness follows from the elliptic regularity of (-\Delta^*_0)^{-1} and the Rellich–Kondrachov embedding.

Appendix B: Non-Uniqueness of the Grad–Shafranov Equation

Ham and Farrell, Nucl. Fusion 64 (2024) 034001 (DOI: 10.1088/1741-4326/ad1d77), established that the Grad–Shafranov equation "for axisymmetric MHD equilibria is a nonlinear, scalar PDE which in principle can have zero, one or more non-trivial solutions". They developed "a simple analytic model to calculate multiple solutions in the large aspect ratio limit" and compared the results to the deflated continuation method in realistic geometry using the finite element method. The analytic model is "surprisingly accurate in calculating multiple solutions of the GSE for given boundary conditions and the two methods agree well in limiting cases". They examined the effect of plasma shaping and aspect ratio and "show that shaping generally does not alter the number of solutions". For a nonlinear source profile of the form \mathcal{F}(\psi) = C_1 - \tanh((\psi - \psi_p)/w), a fold bifurcation occurs at a critical value C^* \approx 2.7, above which at least three distinct solutions exist: the trivial solution and two non-trivial branches.

Pentland et al., Nucl. Fusion 65 (2025) 086053 (DOI: 10.1088/1741-4326/adf3cc), extended this analysis to the static forward free-boundary problem in real tokamak geometry. Using the validated evolutive equilibrium solver FreeGSNKE and the deflated continuation algorithm, they "discover multiple solutions to the static forward free-boundary GS problem in the MAST-U tokamak geometry" and "identify and characterise distinct equilibrium solutions, including both deeply and more shallowly confined plasma states". They suggest that "the existence of even more equilibria is likely prohibited by the restrictive nature of the integral free-boundary condition, which globally couples poloidal fluxes on the computational boundary with those on the interior". This result has direct implications for the GOEPP architecture: the toroidal core must be designed with explicit branch selection criteria, and the stability of each branch must be analysed separately.

Appendix C: Coupled-Mode Theory and Kirchhoff Circuit Equivalence

For m magnetically coupled series LC resonators, Kirchhoff's voltage law gives

V_s = I_1 \left( R_1 + i \left[ \omega L_1 - \frac{1}{\omega C_1} \right] \right) - i \omega M_{12} I_2 - \cdots - i \omega M_{1n} I_n,

and analogous equations for the other circuits. The mutual inductance is computed via Neumann's formula

M = \frac{\mu}{4\pi} \oint \frac{dl_p \cdot dl_q}{R_{pq}} \quad \text{for } p \neq q,

where dl_p and dl_q are the line elements of the coils and R_{pq} is the distance between them. The power transfer is found by solving the coupled system for the current in the m-th circuit, and the transfer function is

S_{21} = \frac{2 I_m \sqrt{R_f R_m}}{V_s}.

In the coupled-mode formalism, the two-resonator system with resonant frequency \omega, loss rate \Gamma = \omega/(2Q), and coupling coefficient \kappa has eigenmodes with eigenvalues \lambda_\pm = -\Gamma \pm i\sqrt{\kappa^2 - \Gamma^2}. The maximum power transfer efficiency under optimal load is

\eta_{\max} = \frac{U^2}{\left(1 + \sqrt{1 + U^2}\right)^2},

where U = \kappa/\Gamma = k\sqrt{Q_1 Q_2} is the strong-coupling factor and k = M/\sqrt{L_1 L_2} is the coupling coefficient. In the strong-coupling regime U \gg 1, \eta_{\max} \to 1; in the weak-coupling regime U \ll 1, \eta_{\max} \approx U^2/4. This result is standard in the wireless power transfer literature and has been demonstrated experimentally by Kurs et al., Science 317 (2007) 83–86, who transferred "60 watts with approximately 40% efficiency over distances in excess of 2 meters".

The Kirchhoff circuit and coupled-mode frameworks are "approximately equivalent with some distinctions". The coupled-mode approach is "only applicable to coils with high quality factor (Q) and large coupling distance". The formal statement of the regime dichotomy is:

Lemma (Regime Dichotomy). Let d_{\text{NF}} = \lambda/2\pi. If d < d_{\text{NF}} and Q > 100, then k is defined and the efficiency is given by \eta_{\max} = U^2/(1 + \sqrt{1 + U^2})^2. If d > d_{\text{NF}}, then k is undefined and power transfer is governed by the Friis equation P_r = P_t G_t G_r (\lambda/4\pi L)^2. No interpolation between regimes is permitted.

Appendix D: Plasma Dielectric Response and Quantitative Exclusion

The plasma dielectric function with electron-ion collisions is

\varepsilon(\omega) = 1 - \frac{\sigma(\omega)}{i\omega\varepsilon_0} = 1 - \frac{\omega_{pl}^2}{\omega(\omega + i\nu_{ei})},

where \omega_{pl} is the plasma frequency and \nu_{ei} is the electron-ion collision frequency. The dispersion relation is k^2 c^2 = \omega^2 \varepsilon(\omega). In the high-frequency limit \omega \gg \nu_{ei}, the real part of the frequency satisfies

\omega_r = \sqrt{k^2 c^2 + \omega_{pl}^2},

and the damping rate is

\nu_E = \frac{\omega_{pl}^2}{\omega_r^2} \nu_{ei}.

For a plasma column with electron density n_e = 10^{16}–10^{17} cm⁻³ = 10^{22}–10^{23} m⁻³, the plasma frequency is

\omega_p = \sqrt{\frac{n_e e^2}{\varepsilon_0 m_e}} \approx 5.6 \times 10^{12} \text{ rad/s}.

For a ground tower operating at f = 100–500 kHz, \omega_{\text{tower}} = 6.28 \times 10^5–3.14 \times 10^6 rad/s. The ratio \omega_{\text{tower}}/\omega_p < 10^{-4}. The dielectric function is negative and large in magnitude: \varepsilon \approx -\omega_p^2/\omega^2 \approx -10^{14}. The electromagnetic penetration depth is

\delta = \frac{c}{\sqrt{\omega_p^2 - \omega^2}} \approx \frac{c}{\omega_p} \approx 50 \;\mu\text{m}.

Power coupling through a plasma column of thickness d \gg \delta is bounded by \exp(-2d/\delta), which is analytically zero. The collisional correction gives a DC conductivity \sigma_{\text{dc}} = n_e e^2/(m_e \nu) \approx 2.8 S/m for \nu = 10^9 s⁻¹, and the ratio of absorbed power to tower power is

\frac{P_{\text{abs}}}{P_{\text{tower}}} < 10^{-6}.

Exclusion is quantitative, not merely cutoff-based. Any architecture asserting tower-driven plasma sustainment at 100–500 kHz violates the cold-plasma cutoff condition and is analytically excluded.

Appendix E: Filamentation Critical Power and Multiphoton Ionisation

The critical power for self-focusing in air at 1030 nm is

P_{\text{crit}} = \frac{\lambda^2}{2\pi n_0 n_2},

where n_2 \approx 3 \times 10^{-19} cm²/W for air, giving P_{\text{crit}} \approx 5.3 GW for 7 ps pulses. The multiphoton order for O₂ at 1030 nm is determined by the ratio of the ionisation potential to the photon energy: photon energy = 1.2 eV, O₂ ionisation = 12.07 eV, giving

K = \left\lceil \frac{12.07}{1.2} \right\rceil = 11.

The filamentation length bound is

L_f \leq \frac{U_0}{\bar{\beta} I_0^{K-1}},

consistent with the established scaling L_f \propto \lambda a_0^{-2} \sqrt{P/P_{\text{crit}}} from Couairon and Mysyrowicz, Phys. Rep. 441 (2007) 47–189. The intensity at the filament core is saturated at approximately 10^{13} W/cm². When the input power greatly exceeds the critical power, the beam splits into multiple filaments, and the interaction between filaments during propagation is complex. The 4 \times 4 array projection L \leq 20 m is stated as a conjecture, not a theorem, and requires proof of no destructive interference between filaments.

Appendix F: Gaussian Beam Collection Efficiency

For a Gaussian beam with transmit aperture radius a_t and wavelength \lambda propagating distance L to a receive aperture radius a_r, the collection efficiency is

\eta_{\text{coll}} = 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{2a_r^2}{w^2(L)}\right),

where w(L) = w_0\sqrt{1 + (\lambda L/\pi w_0^2)^2} is the beam radius at distance L. The diffraction-limited beam divergence is \theta \approx 1.22 \lambda / D_t. For D_t = 10 m and \lambda = 5.17 cm (5.8 GHz), \theta = 6.3 mrad. The beam radius at L = 550 km is w(L) = L\theta/\sqrt{2} \approx 2.45 km. For 90% collection, a_r > 1.07 w = 2.6 km. Matching the receiver radius to the beam radius captures approximately 87% of the total power, and beyond this point, the power gains become less significant compared to the required increase in receiver diameter.

Appendix G: Three-Tier Efficiency Budget for Orbital Power Beaming

The end-to-end efficiency of a microwave power-beaming link is the product of component efficiencies:

\eta_{\text{total}} = \eta_{\text{DC-RF}} \cdot \eta_{\text{coll}} \cdot \eta_{\text{atm}} \cdot \eta_{\text{RF-DC}}.

Tier 1 (Ideal). Using ideal values \eta_{\text{DC-RF}} \approx 0.9, \eta_{\text{coll}} \approx 0.9, \eta_{\text{atm}} \approx 0.99, \eta_{\text{RF-DC}} \approx 0.9, this gives \eta_{\text{ideal}} \approx 0.72.

Tier 2 (Achievable). Using realistic component values \eta_{\text{DC-RF}} \approx 0.5, \eta_{\text{coll}} \approx 0.87, \eta_{\text{atm}} \approx 0.95–0.99, \eta_{\text{RF-DC}} \approx 0.6, this gives \eta_{\text{achievable}} \approx 0.05–0.15.

Tier 3 (System). The Caltech space solar power analysis, published in Joule (2025), DOI: 10.1016/j.joule.2025.101928, reports a 113 MW system with 559 modules at an LCOE of 9.4 ¢/kWh. The system-level efficiency progresses from 2.77% (current) to 4.14% (mid-term) to 6.63% (long-term) as the technology matures. The Caltech analysis estimates a total lifecycle cost of approximately US$983 million over 15 years. The calibration must be against Tier 2, with the gap to Tier 3 analysed as a separate systems-engineering loss.


